WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.497
Lesson two.

00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:03.792
The pilot is a neural network.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.234
But evolution never touches the network directly.

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:10.916
It works on a genome, a recipe for building one.

00:00:11.516 --> 00:00:14.769
Here is the network that evolution found, flying.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:17.336
The eight inputs come in on the left.

00:00:17.336 --> 00:00:20.589
Each connection multiplies a value by its weight.

00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:25.839
Orange connections are positive, blue ones are negative, and thicker means stronger.

00:00:25.839 --> 00:00:31.617
Each node adds up what comes in, and squashes it into a value between minus one and one.

00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:35.310
On the right are four scores, one per action.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.112
The action with the highest score fires.

00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:42.731
Watch the scores change from moment to moment as the lander flies.

00:00:43.331 --> 00:00:48.899
This network has no hidden nodes at all, just seventeen of the thirty-two possible connections.

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:50.143
Nobody drew it.

00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:53.873
Its shape and every one of its weights came out of evolution.

00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:57.633
We will see why it stayed this small in the next lesson.

00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:00.337
The genome is a list of genes.

00:01:00.337 --> 00:01:02.718
Node genes say which neurons exist.

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:09.749
Connection genes say which two neurons are wired together, with what weight, and whether the connection is switched on.

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:14.755
Read the list from top to bottom, and you can build the network on the right.

00:01:15.355 --> 00:01:20.365
Each connection gene also carries a historical marker, called its innovation number.

00:01:20.365 --> 00:01:27.009
It is handed out the first time that connection appears anywhere in the population, and every later copy keeps it.

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:31.094
So if two genomes share a number, those genes have a common origin.

00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:35.668
This small idea is what makes crossover work, as we will see in lesson four.

00:01:35.668 --> 00:01:42.013
The library we use, neat-python, names each gene by the two nodes it connects, which does the same job.
